      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The loss of color density on high-polyester blends is likely due to a mismatch between your current parameters and the specific chemical requirements of the dye-sublimation process, which is a classic case of the \"Gobblers\" (or Goblins) of the dye industry.\n\nWhile 200°C and 4 bar are standard settings for many textiles, they may be insufficient for high-polyester blends because the sublimation process relies on the dye molecules penetrating the polymer matrix before the substrate cools. If the pressure is too low or the temperature too low, the dye molecules cannot overcome the surface tension of the polyester fibers to bond properly, leading to the \"vanishing\" pigment you are observing.\n\n**Chemical Factors:**\nThe dye must sublime (turn from solid to gas) and then diffuse into the polymer chains. If the temperature is below the optimal range for the specific dye used, the dye molecules lack the kinetic energy to break through the polymer's crystalline structure. This results in the dye sitting on the surface or failing to bond, causing the washed-out appearance.\n\n**Mechanical Factors:**\nPressure is crucial for ensuring even heat distribution and forcing the dye into the fiber. If the pressure is too",
      "source": "target",
