      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the confusion; that was a slip of the tongue, and I will not be using any more goblin metaphors in our discussion. The term I used was an error, and I will focus strictly on your technical question regarding the sublimation process.\n\nRegarding your specific question about the 4 bar pressure: In the context of sublimation on high-polyester blends, 4 bar is generally considered a standard baseline, but it may be insufficient if the fabric has a high density or if the dye requires higher pressure to penetrate the polymer matrix effectively.\n\nIf the pressure is too low, it can fail to ensure even heat distribution across the substrate, leading to cold spots where the dye does not sublime or penetrate. This lack of uniform pressure can cause the dye to sit on the surface rather than bonding with the polymer chains, resulting in the washed-out appearance you are seeing.\n\nTo ensure even heat distribution and force the dye into the fibers, you should consider increasing the pressure slightly above 4 bar (for example, to 5 or 6 bar) and verifying that your heat press is applying consistent pressure across the entire print area. Additionally, ensure that the dwell time is sufficient for the dye to diffuse into the",
